%I A399300 #7 Sep 01 2026 18:43:03
%S A399300 6,10,22,114,114,120,44286,90,120,1116452670
%N A399300 a(n) is the least even number whose n-th smallest Goldbach prime equals A398545(n).
%C A399300 For even N, sort the primes p <= N/2 with N - p prime in increasing order: the n-th one is the n-th smallest Goldbach prime of N. A398545(n) is the least value the congruence conditions permit it to take; a(n) is the least even number attaining that value.
%C A399300 Not monotone: a(8) = 90 precedes a(7) = 44286. The Goldbach primes of 90 begin 7, 11, 17, 19, 23, 29, 31, 37, so its 8th smallest is 37 = A398545(8), while its 7th is 31 rather than 29 = A398545(7); realizing 29 exactly in 7th position first happens at N = 44286.
%C A399300 a(4) = a(5) = 114 and a(6) = a(9) = 120.
%C A399300 For n = 10, 11, 12, no even N <= 10^8 attains A398545(n). Under the Hardy-Littlewood prime k-tuple conjecture, a(n) exists for all n.
%e A399300 a(4) = 114: the Goldbach partitions of 114 begin 5 + 109, 7 + 107, 11 + 103, 13 + 101, so the 4th smallest Goldbach prime of 114 is 13 = A398545(4), and no smaller even number has 4th smallest Goldbach prime equal to 13.
%o A399300 (Python)
%o A399300 from sympy import isprime, primerange
%o A399300 A398545 = [3, 5, 11, 13, 17, 23, 29, 37, 41]  # terms with proven attainment
%o A399300 def kth(N, k):
%o A399300     c = 0
%o A399300     for p in primerange(3, N//2 + 1):
%o A399300         if isprime(N - p):
%o A399300             c += 1
%o A399300             if c == k: return p
%o A399300     return 0
%o A399300 def a(n):  # valid for n <= 9; for n >= 10 no attainment is known below 10^8
%o A399300     N = 6
%o A399300     while kth(N, n) != A398545[n-1]: N += 2
%o A399300     return N
%o A399300 print([a(n) for n in range(1, 10)])
%Y A399300 Cf. A020481, A398183, A398545.
